-- title:
--   Largest dominant index equals minus the branch order
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative domain which is a discrete valuation ring, complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal, let $\varpi \in W$ be irreducible, and let $e, q \ge 1$ be integers. Write $R =$ `UVCrossingModel W (ϖ ^ e)`, the quotient of $W[[X_0,X_1]]$ (two-variable power series indexed by `Fin 2`) by the ideal generated by $X_0 X_1 - \varpi^e$, and let `mk (ϖ ^ e)` be the quotient map, with `U`, `V` the images of $X_0$, $X_1$ and `const (ϖ ^ e) w` the image of the constant $w$. Let $x \in R$ be nonzero, and let $ab = (a,b)$ be a pair of one-variable power series over $W$ with $b$ of zero constant term, such that $x$ is the image of `inU a + inV b`, the two-variable series whose coefficient at $d$ is the $d_0$-th coefficient of $a$ when $d_1 = 0$ and the $d_1$-th coefficient of $b$ when $d_0 = 0$. Assume further that for some unit $\gamma$ of $R$ and some $n \in \mathbb{N}$ one has $x - \gamma V^n \in (\varpi, U)R$. Then, for the valuation function $w \mapsto q \cdot \mathrm{addVal}_W(w)$ with values in $\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$ and for both weight parameters equal to $q e$: the Gauss order of $x$, namely the supremum over all two-variable representatives $F$ of $x$ of $\inf_d \bigl(q\,\mathrm{addVal}_W(\mathrm{coeff}_d F) + \mathrm{annulusWeight}\,(qe)\,(qe)\,d\bigr)$, is $0$; and the supremum of the set of dominant indices of $ab$ — those integers $m$ for which $q\,\mathrm{addVal}_W(\mathrm{nfCoeff}\,ab\,m) + \mathrm{annulusWeight}\,(qe)\,(qe)\,(\mathrm{nfExponent}\,m)$ attains the value $\inf_d \bigl(q\,\mathrm{addVal}_W(\mathrm{coeff}_d(\mathrm{inU}\,a + \mathrm{inV}\,b)) + \mathrm{annulusWeight}\,(qe)\,(qe)\,d\bigr)$ — equals $-n$ in $\mathbb{Z}$.
--
--   This is the end-point form of the principle that the slope with which the Gauss-order polygon of a function on a node annulus arrives at an end equals minus the order of vanishing of its reduction along the corresponding branch of the special fibre: the ideal $(\varpi, U)$ cuts out that branch, and modulo it $x$ becomes a unit times $V^n$. It is used in the node-annulus machinery that identifies the order of a residue on a branch with the extremal dominant index, and in the construction of component charts and annuli for prolongation tuples on modular curves.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_UVCrossingModel_sSup_dominantIndices_eq_neg_of_sub_mul_V_pow_mem.lean

